lif contains some Arabic influence  some Arabic loanwords include said I'm a  queen the traditional Islamic greeting  some of the days of the week that  Tuesday from Arabic at the lava lava  Wednesday from Arabic RBR  nice first day from Arabic Alchemist and  ajumma Friday from Arabic l-jamaa last  time or our this is from Arabic walked  or walk - which includes the Arabic  nominative case ending ooh there are  some more words but not that many to be  honest there has also been French  influence on Woollett  that's because Senegal is a former  French colony the French established  trading outposts there beginning in the  17th century and fully conquered Senegal  during the 19th century it remained a  French colonial territory until 1960 the  Dakar variety of wool off' has the most  french influence with numerous French  loanwords the rural varieties of wool  off' are more conservative we'll see  some examples of French loanwords later  some features of Wolof the semi Gambian  languages including Wolof do not have  tones this sets them apart from most of  the Niger Congo language family like  other languages in its family wellif  contains pre nasalized consonants these  are combinations of nasal sounds and  stops which function as individual  consonants like most Niger Congo  languages  Wolof has a system of noun classes  categories of different types of nouns  that are represented by specific  inflections but Wolof is unique in that  the noun itself is not inflected but  rather it's typically the definite  article that is inflected for example  the shirt seam is V the cat moves me the  woman aging G the earth soo see the noun  class inflection can be seen in the  first consonant of the definite article  there are ten noun classes including two  plural classes though I understand that  in urban wool ax b has replaced the  others to a large extent wolf has no  separate grammatical category of  adjectives instead there's a category of  verbs called stative verbs which  indicate states conditions feelings etc  these are used instead of adjectives  Wolof has three sets of personal  pronouns independent pronouns object  pronouns and subject pronouns  independent pronouns are used for  emphasis and to answer questions similar  to the disjunctive pronouns in French  independent pronouns and object pronouns  have  fixed forms but subject pronouns have  many different forms in the wolf verb  system the verb itself is not conjugated  but rather the subject pronoun is  inflected instead the subject pronouns  indicate not only person a number but  can also indicate time normally aspect  rather than tense as well as focus it  can be verb focus object focus or  subject focus there are three types of  verbs in Wolof action verbs identity  verbs and state of verbs which we  mentioned before identity verbs are  verbs that give basic information about  you and your background such as doot to  be named yoga to come from you do to be  born and so on these three types of  verbs are important because they're used  in different verbal constructions  different conjugations if you will all  right let's jump right into some  sentences and see some of these features  in action you can go to do the sentence  means what's your name word for word  it's how you singular named Linda is an  object focus pronoun it's focusing our  attention on the word naka meaning how  so it's often used in asking or  answering questions that identify the  object or compliment this here is the  second person singular object focus  pronoun to be clear there's a full set  of these object focus pronouns for the  various persons and numbers these are  subject pronouns but they have the added  function of telling us to focus on or  emphasize the object this is one set of  subject pronouns with that specific  function but there are numerous other  sets of subject pronouns with different  functions here too is the identity verb  meaning to be from note that this  question is often contracted to know to  do with know being a contraction of naka  and gaw but I gave you the full form to  show how the underlying sentence is  constructed an answer might be good  attitude word-for-word it  goroh that's a name I named in this  reply la is the first person singular  object focus pronoun one thing to note  is that will of typically follows an SVO  word order but in an object focused  sentence with a verb it's osv to give a  greater sense of the object focus maybe  an English  we could translate this as it is gora  that I am called here's another sentence  with object focus simile Galib Duke this  means I love my job or it is my job that  I love maybe you'd say this in response  to a question asking what you love about  your life now let's make this a subject  focus sentence na bakke somebody K this  means I love my job with the emphasis on  the subject I maybe you'd say this if  someone asks who loves their job  overhearing your conversation now let's  make this a verb focus sentence then of  a summary key this means I love my job  with the emphasis on love maybe you say  this in response to someone asking if  you dislike your job notice again that  in the object focus sentence the object  comes at the beginning also remember  that these are subject pronouns which  include the additional meaning of focus  these ones here are only the first  person singular forms which are part of  wider sets of pronouns this form here  indicates verb focus for action verbs  but when it's a state of verb that  functions like an adjective  then this form draws our attention to a  state or condition does it end it's hot  here Tunde is a state of verb meaning to  be hot this construction is often used  to explain a reason or cause let's look  at another sentence meaning dick this  means they're having breakfast this  sentence contains a different kind of  pronoun called a presentative pronoun  these pronouns don't have any focus but  rather indicate an aspect of time they  