ntence to avoid that confusion  when the perfective is used with the  state of verb it doesn't indicate a past  state it indicates a present state the  perfective indicates that the  development of that state is completed  notice it assumed a body this sentence  means I already have a reservation  word-for-word 'its have i perfective  reservation already note the French word  reservation here you can see that a  present state is expressed by the  perfective form now if we want to  express a past state using a state of  verb we can add a special past marker  not a service on my body this means I  already had a reservation  rather than have a reservation another  sentence hey Luke Dean nu den masa B  this means tomorrow we'll go to the  market word for word it tomorrow we  imperfect go market la in the sentence  we see a different type of subject  pronoun that indicates future well to be  precise  it's the imperfective and indicates that  an action has not been completed yet  here we have the word for market which  comes from the French word marche and  it's followed by the definite article  for its noun class this one starting  with a B the imperfect of pronouns do  not always express future if we add to  them the suffix E which expresses  continuous action that the imperfect of  pronouns indicate a habitual action  which would be expressed with the simple  present tense in English CNN New Eden  Marcy B mezzo Nick  this means we go to the market every day  here we see an imperfect of pronoun with  the prefix e which makes this a habitual  action like we go rather than a future  action like we will go in the phrase  base panic base means day and bunich  means each or every let's look at one  last sentence a ye alone onion Mundi was  done this means the children aren't  sleeping they're chatting word for word  its children the sleep negative they  continuous chat this sentence is about  actions that are in progress right now  so here we see the presentative pronoun  ng which indicates ongoing action that  they are chatting right now closer to  the beginning of the sentence we have  this verb neeli which means sleep with a  negative suffix but no presentative  pronoun this negative suffix indicates  person and number third-person plural  but it doesn't contain any information  about time or focus it's simply negative  this is the normal way of expressing the  negative for all types of verb  constructions so the time and focus  elements are understood from context  here at the beginning we see the phrase  Haleh E which means the children yi is a  definite article for plural nouns  there's one more definite article for  plural nouns me but that one is quite  limited in use these plural articles are  two of the ten definite articles that  are marked for noun class one last thing  to note is the word Rakatan the root of  this word is hua which means to speak  and the suffix tun changes the meaning  to chat and it can also be a noun  meaning conversation or chat but there  are numerous other words built from this  root there is also meaning to have some  speak the causative form Mossad which  means to haggle or to bargain lacet  which means to repeat or restate and  last to which means to talk to yourself  and there's also a homonym of this word  meaning time or hour which comes from  the Arabic word walk do as you can see  wool off' is a very interesting language  and one that might work quite  differently from your native language or  any other language that you've studied  before  perhaps it's standout feature is its  verbal system which makes extensive use  of inflected pronouns which work  together with largely unchangeable verbs  to create a rich system of expression in  this video we merely scratched the  surface and there are more constructions  that indicate different times aspects  and moods and there are additional  features of the language that are beyond  the scope of this video but hopefully it  sparked your interest and 